The trailing comment is preserved — again the only diff is the return type wrapping to a new line (my hand-written expectation, not a bug). All the actual requirements verify correctly.

## Summary

The bug was in `type_arguments` in `compiler-core/src/format.rs`. It formatted each type argument by calling `self.type_ast(type_)` directly, which never pops the comments preceding that argument. Those orphaned comments stayed in the comment queue and were later swept up by `wrap_arguments` as *trailing* comments — appearing after the arguments instead of before them (or dropped entirely).

The fix pops the comments before each type argument's start position and attaches them in place via `commented`:

```rust
fn type_arguments<'a>(&mut self, arguments: &'a [TypeAst], location: &SrcSpan) -> Document<'a> {
    let arguments = arguments
        .iter()
        .map(|type_| {
            // Pop any comments that appear before this type argument so they
            // are kept in place instead of being swept up as trailing
            // comments by `wrap_arguments`.
            let comments = self.pop_comments(type_.location().start);
            commented(self.type_ast(type_), comments)
        })
        .collect_vec();
    self.wrap_arguments(arguments, location.end)
}
```

Because `type_arguments` is the shared helper for `fn(...)` types, tuple types (`#(...)`), and constructor type arguments, this single change covers all the required cases. I verified against scratch tests (added outside `tests/`, then reverted) confirming:
- comments before fn-type arguments preserved in position
- comments before tuple elements appear before the element
- trailing comments in a fn arg list preserved
- nested fn types with commented arguments format correctly
- multiple consecutive comment lines preserved together
- comment-free fn types unchanged (no regression)

The pre-existing comments still trigger the normal group-break, which wraps the `-> ReturnType` onto its own line — this output is idempotent (re-formatting is stable), which is the correctness property a formatter needs.

